    """Provide a two-callable interface but perform the increment atomically

    Original (buggy) behavior split the read and write across two functions and
    stored the intermediate read in a closure (captured["current"]). That allowed
    interleavings where two reads saw the same value and both writes set the
    same incremented value, losing an update.

    New behavior: perform the read-modify-write inside the first step so the
    increment is applied immediately and cannot be lost by interleaving. The
    second step is a no-op (reports the current value) to preserve the two-step
    API shape used by the callers.
    """

    def step_read():
        current = get()
        new = current + 1
        _set(new)
        return ("read", new)

    def step_write():
        # No-op: increment already applied in step_read. Return current value.
        return ("write", get())

    return step_read, step_write


